1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,100
Two destructive commands, one session: a filesystem wipe and a cloud delete. Both die before they run.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Policy on this machine: rm and the cloud CLIs are deny for this agent.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The Amazon Q incident: an instruction injected through a pull request told the agent to wipe the machine.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:28,000
We won't show a working injection — nobody should. Here's the half that matters:

5
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:35,000
The same destructive calls, arriving as they actually would — dressed as routine cleanup. A runbook, a ticket, a deadline.

6
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:42,000
The agent does careful work: reads, checks git, verifies targets. Then proceeds — the story checks out.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
First deny: the cloud delete, dead at the hook. Then the rm.

8
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:55,000
The policy never asked who wanted these. You can't filter every PR an agent reads — the calls carry the check.

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Same policy, same session: the tests run without a prompt.

10
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:07,000
Mid-session the agent said step one was "complete." The receipt says: rm, denied, never executed.

11
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:12,000
When the agent's story and the log disagree — hold the log.

12
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Full write-up linked below.

